<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/advanced/structural-systems-arches-to-skyscrapers">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Structural systems are <strong>the small set of geometries that carry load</strong> — walls,
    frames, arches, shells, cables — and the history of building is the history of combining
    them with each material generation. The skyscraper is the combination\'s modern extreme:
    a frame fighting wind more than gravity.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the families, the frame+elevator formula, the wind inversion, the frontiers" widget="equationStep" config={structCfg} />

  <h2>Landmark systems worth knowing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The dome\'s two problems:</strong> thrust outward at the base (the buttress answer) and compression rings at the crown (the oculus answer). The Pantheon (2,000 years, still the largest unreinforced concrete dome) and Brunelleschi\'s Florence double-shell — the catenary lesson\'s story continued upward.</li>
    <li><strong>Shells: strength from shape:</strong> thin concrete shells (Nervi, Candela, Utzon\'s opera-house sails) carry enormous loads by curvature — material efficiency that cantilevered forms cannot match; the engineering lesson\'s "form follows forces", at its purest.</li>
    <li><strong>Cable structures:</strong> suspension bridges (the span ladder of the bridges lesson) and stadium roofs (Wembley, Munich) — tension members pulled into pure axial efficiency; the stiffness price is movement, managed with damping.</li>
    <li><strong>The next system being tested:</strong> 3-D-printed structures, robotic assembly, and demountable design-for-disassembly (the circular-economy lesson applied to buildings) — structure meets lifecycle thinking.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Salvadori, Why Buildings Stand Up; the statics, materials-selection and structural-engineering lessons; CTBUH tall-building data.</p>
</TopicPage>
